On Dec 14, 2011, at 7:30 PM, Dario Strbenac wrote:

> Hello,
> 
> Using the JRI API, what's the right way to create a data.frame in R, from a List in Java ? I tried following Oliver Mannion's design pattern in jamsim of creating column arrays and doing rList.put() but the API has changed, and the constructor for RList he used no longer exists, and either does the put method for an RList object.
> 
> Say that I already have my columns in Java arrays :
> 
> String[] colNames = {"chr", "position"};
> String[] chrs = {"chr1", "chr2", "chr3"};
> Integer[] pos = {1000, 2000, 5000};
> 
> How do I get from there to a data.frame using the instance of Rengine I have created ?
> 

			    String[] colNames = {"chr", "position"};
			    String[] chrs = {"chr1", "chr2", "chr3"};
			    int[] pos = {1000, 2000, 5000};

			    REXP df = new REXPGenericVector(new RList(new REXP[] { new REXPString(chrs), new REXPInteger(pos) }, colNames));
			    eng.assign("df", df);
			    eng.parseAndEval("print(as.data.frame(df))");


   chr position
1 chr1     1000
2 chr2     2000
3 chr3     5000


If you don't want to use as.data.frame() on the list, you have to do some extra work to create the "class" and "row.names" attributes.

Cheers,
Simon

On Dec 18, 2011, at 7:00 PM, Dario Strbenac wrote:

> In R, it's possible to give it incomplete expressions, and it will wait for more input before running. Can this be done with JRI ?
> 
> Can something like this work as expected ?
> 
>            rExec.eval("x <- numeric()");
>            rExec.eval("for(i in 1:5)");
>            rExec.eval("x <- c(x, i)");
>            System.out.println(rExec.eval("print(x)"));
> 

By definition, no. R can only evaluate complete expressions. Even in the R console the expression is not evaluated until it is complete (i.e. when you type "for(i in 1:10)" in R it is *not* evaluated since it doesn't parse into an expression).

Cheers,
Simon

